id: rust.send-sync
kind: rust
title: Send and Sync
aliases: Send, Sync, marker traits
tags: concurrency, fundamentals
questions:
  - what do Send and Sync mean?
invariant: Send allows moving a value to another thread; Sync allows sharing references from another thread (so &T is Send).
answer_30s: Send means ownership can cross a thread boundary and Sync means shared references can; both are auto traits derived from the fields.
answer_2m: |
  The useful skill is reading the negative case. Rc is neither Send nor Sync
  (non-atomic counter); Cell and RefCell are Send but not Sync (interior
  mutability without synchronisation); a std MutexGuard is not Send, which is
  exactly why holding one across an await breaks tokio::spawn. When the compiler
  reports that a future is not Send, remove the non-Send value from the future
  (usually by shrinking a guard's scope) -- never with `unsafe impl Send`.
when_to_use:
  - reading compiler errors about futures, spawn and thread boundaries
minimal_example: |
  fn assert_send<T: Send>(_: &T) {}
  fn assert_sync<T: Sync>(_: &T) {}

  assert_send(&std::sync::Arc::new(1u32));
  assert_sync(&std::sync::Arc::new(1u32));
  // assert_send(&std::rc::Rc::new(1u32)); // Rc is not Send
failure_modes:
  - silencing a not-Send error with unsafe impl Send, hiding a data race
  - assuming RefCell is fine because single-threaded tests compile
related:
  - rust.arc
  - rust.mutex-across-await
  - rust.tokio-spawn
sources:
  - https://doc.rust-lang.org/nomicon/send-and-sync.html
